# Erc7984

_Reference for zama-solidity skill (zama-protocol plugin)_

# ERC-7984 Confidential Tokens

- [ERC-7984](https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-7984) defines the interface and explains its design.
- [OpenZeppelin Confidential Contracts](https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-confidential-contracts) implement it for FHEVM. Build on them, and read the installed package source or the [OpenZeppelin docs](https://docs.openzeppelin.com/confidential-contracts/token) for the exact API. Do not write your own encrypted balances, allowances or transfers.

## How it differs from ERC-20

- **Balances and amounts are handles.** Only accounts with access can decrypt them.
- **Transfers return what moved.** The standard lets a transfer move less than requested. OpenZeppelin's token cannot see an insufficient balance, so it moves an encrypted zero instead of reverting. Use the returned amount, never the requested one.
- **Operators replace allowances.** An allowance would need an amount to track. Instead the holder calls `setOperator(spender, until)`, with `until` in Unix seconds, and the operator can move any amount until then. It gets temporary access to the amount it moved. Reading the holder's balance needs a separate access grant.
- **Transfer-and-call notifies the receiver.** The receiver's callback returns an encrypted success flag. If it returns false, the token refunds the sender and the returned amount is zero. The refund is best-effort: a receiver that spends the tokens during the callback keeps them.

## Rules

- **Move real tokens.** A payment, escrow or vault must transfer tokens. Encrypted numbers recorded beside a token are not custody.
- **Pull only from the caller.** An operator contract calls `confidentialTransferFrom(msg.sender, ...)`, never from an address the caller names, or anyone can spend every holder who approved it.
- **Check who calls your callback.** A receiver callback must check that `msg.sender` is the token it expects: anyone can call it directly. Callbacks run outside code during the transfer, so guard against reentrancy.
- **Use the token's units.** Amounts are `euint64`. OpenZeppelin's default is 6 decimals. Extensions can differ. A wrapper's `decimals()` and `rate()` can differ from its ERC-20's. Use integers only.
- **Name overloads explicitly from ethers.** Transfer functions have one overload for an external input with proof and one for an existing handle.

Find deployed wrappers in the registry (**zama-protocol** → `references/addresses.md`). For deployed payment and escrow designs, read `code-map.md`.
